In article <11989@pt.cs.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ECE.CMU.EDU (David Hairston) writes:
>let me say that polypiling is _guaranteed_ to work (if used properly, the
>polymorph reaction is unstable and breeds more wands).

This would be EXTREMELY easy to fix.  The code is currently (3.0i) to
avoid polypiling into wands of wishing is:

if (otmp2->otyp == WAN_WISHING)
  while (otmp2->otyp == WAN_WISHING || otmp2->otyp == WAN_POLYMORPH)
    otmp2->otyp = rnd_class(WAN_LIGHT, WAN_LIGHTNING)

This sezs that if you get a wand of wishing, change it into another
wand that isn't a wand of polymorph or wand of polymorph.  If the
first line was:

if (otmp2->otyp == WAN_WISHING || otmp2->otyp == WAN_POLYMORPH)

The problem of getting more wands of polymorph would be instantly
fixed.

Something more sophisticated, but perhaps an even better idea, would
be to make wands of polymorph, striking, and teleportation, when
hitting a pile, only affect the top few things on the pile.  Say, make
it random (say, 5-10).  I don't know how this code works, but piles
must be stored on linked lists.  You'd have to add a count to the code
for stepping through, and only step through a certain amount.

The two of these, taken together would mean that you can only get just
so many objects polymorphed with any one wand.  You could still
polypile, but only a limited amount of objects.  This would leave in
an interesting piece of the game, without making it too easy to win
once you find the right kind of wand.

Any objections?  The first change is so easy and obvious, I can't see
why it shouldn't be done immediately.

